Appendix A: Mathematical Derivations and Proofs

A.1 Leg Kinematics and Jacobian

The Denavit‑Hartenberg (DH) parameters for a single 6‑DOF leg are given in Table A.1. The forward kinematics map from the hip frame $\{H_i\}$ to the foot frame $\{F_i\}$ is computed as the product of the six link transformations:

\mathbf{T}_{F_i}^{H_i}(\mathbf{q}_i) = \prod_{j=1}^{6} \begin{bmatrix} \cos\theta_j & -\sin\theta_j\cos\alpha_j & \sin\theta_j\sin\alpha_j & a_j\cos\theta_j \\ \sin\theta_j & \cos\theta_j\cos\alpha_j & -\cos\theta_j\sin\alpha_j & a_j\sin\theta_j \\ 0 & \sin\alpha_j & \cos\alpha_j & d_j \\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix} 

The foot velocity $\mathbf{v}_{F_i} = [\dot{x}, \dot{y}, \dot{z}, \omega_x, \omega_y, \omega_z]^\top$ is related to the joint velocities $\dot{\mathbf{q}}_i$ by the geometric Jacobian $\mathbf{J}_i \in \mathbb{R}^{6 \times 6}$:

\mathbf{J}_i = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{\partial \mathbf{p}}{\partial q_1} & \cdots & \frac{\partial \mathbf{p}}{\partial q_6} \\ \mathbf{z}_1 & \cdots & \mathbf{z}_6 \end{bmatrix} 

where $\mathbf{p}$ is the foot position vector and $\mathbf{z}_k$ is the axis of rotation of joint $k$ expressed in the world frame.

A.2 Floating‑Base Dynamics

The equations of motion for the full 30‑dimensional system (6 body DOFs + 24 joint DOFs) are derived from the Lagrangian:

\frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac{\partial L}{\partial \dot{\mathbf{q}}}\right) - \frac{\partial L}{\partial \mathbf{q}} = \mathbf{S}^\top \boldsymbol{\tau} + \sum_{i=1}^{4} \mathbf{J}_{c_i}^\top \mathbf{f}_{c_i} 

where $L = T - V$ is the kinetic minus potential energy. The mass matrix $\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{q})$ appears in the standard form:

\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{q}) \ddot{\mathbf{q}} + \mathbf{C}(\mathbf{q},\dot{\mathbf{q}}) \dot{\mathbf{q}} + \mathbf{g}(\mathbf{q}) = \mathbf{S}^\top \boldsymbol{\tau} + \sum_{i=1}^{4} \mathbf{J}_{c_i}^\top \mathbf{f}_{c_i} 

with $\mathbf{g}(\mathbf{q}) = \partial V / \partial \mathbf{q}$ and $V = \sum_k m_k g_M z_k$, where $g_M = 3.711\,\text{m·s}^{-2}$.

A.3 Static Torque Requirement for Tool Holding

For a payload mass $m_p$ held at the tool frame $\{T\}$, the gravitational wrench in the body frame is $\mathbf{W}_T = [0, 0, -m_p g_M, 0, 0, 0]^\top$. The Jacobian from joint space to tool frame is $\mathbf{J}_T$. By the principle of virtual work, the required joint torques are:

\boldsymbol{\tau} = \mathbf{J}_T^\top \mathbf{W}_T 

Numerical evaluation for $m_p = 200\,\text{kg}$ at maximum horizontal reach gives $\tau_{\text{hip}} \approx 68\,\text{Nm}$ and $\tau_{\text{knee}} \approx 98\,\text{Nm}$.

A.4 Bekker Pressure‑Sinkage Model

The foot–soil interaction is described by the Bekker equation:

p = \left(\frac{k_c}{b} + k_\phi\right) z^n 

For Martian simulant JSC‑Mars‑1, the parameters adopted from the ExoMars rover testbed characterisation are $k_c \approx 0$, $k_\phi = 1400\,\text{kN·m}^{-(n+1)}$, $n = 1.0$. The contact patch width is $b = 0.3\,\text{m}$ (foot diameter). For a vertical load per foot of $F_z = 790\,\text{N}$ (one‑quarter of the rover weight on Mars), the static sinkage is obtained by solving:

p \pi r^2 = F_z \quad \text{with} \quad p = k_\phi z^n 

\Rightarrow z = \frac{F_z}{k_\phi \pi r^2} 

Numerical evaluation gives $z \approx 0.085\,\text{m}$ (8.5 cm). Vibration at 30–50 Hz reduces the effective $k_\phi$ by approximately 40 %, bringing $z$ below 5 cm.

A.5 Carbothermic Reduction Thermodynamics

The endothermic reduction reaction is:

\text{Fe}_2\text{O}_3 + 3\text{C} \rightarrow 2\text{Fe} + 3\text{CO}, \quad \Delta H^\circ_{298} = 492\,\text{kJ·mol}^{-1} 

The theoretical minimum energy per kg of Fe is:

E_{\text{min}} = \frac{492 \times 10^3}{2 \times 55.845 \times 3600} \approx 1.22\,\text{kWh·kg}^{-1} 

The total system energy including MOXIE‑type CO₂ electrolysis and regolith agglomeration is 15.51 MWh per tonne of liquid iron alloy, i.e., 15.51 kWh·kg⁻¹.

