Appendix E: Design Rationale and Trade Studies

E.1 Wheeled vs. Legged Locomotion

The choice of a legged architecture over a wheeled platform is based on:

· Terrain adaptability: legs step over 1.5 m obstacles; wheels are limited to ~30 cm.
· Static stability for tool use: legs lock to form a rigid platform (<0.1 mm vibration); wheeled vehicles require constant suspension movement.
· Omnidirectional movement: legs enable zero‑radius turns; wheeled rovers have non‑holonomic constraints.
· Load distribution: adjustable foot placement optimises ground pressure on loose regolith.

The penalty is higher mechanical complexity and a cost of transport approximately 2–3× that of a wheeled system. This is judged acceptable given the industrial precision requirement.

E.2 On‑board vs. Stationary Smelting

A central design decision was whether to place smelting capability on the rover or in a stationary Foundry Core. The thermodynamic analysis (Appendix A.5) demonstrates that the 15.51 kWh/kg energy cost makes on‑board battery‑powered smelting infeasible. The IC‑1 crucible is retained only as a docked demonstrator tool. All production smelting occurs at the Core, powered by dedicated Kilopower reactors (10 kWe each, 1,500 kg per unit).

E.3 EPM vs. Mechanical Wedge Tool Changer

Electropermanent magnet latches were selected over mechanical wedges for dust tolerance. EPMs have no moving parts, zero holding power, and can be integrated with electrostatic dust shields. Mechanical wedges, while capable of higher precision, are vulnerable to dust jamming and require precise tolerancing. The trade sacrifices ±0.1 mm repeatability for robust operation in Martian dust, resulting in the baseline ±0.5 mm specification.

E.4 Payload Derating

The original AWR‑Mars specification proposed a 400 kg payload. Dynamic analysis (Appendix A.3) shows that, with actuator torques limited to validated levels (70 Nm hip, 120 Nm knee), the safe payload at maximum reach is 200 kg. This derating ensures a factor of safety of 1.5 against static overload and accommodates dynamic transients during locomotion with a tool attached.

